
{0 Launching the server}


To run the server, use the command [ocsigenserver]. It has the
following options:

{v
  DIR, --serve DIR
         Serve the static files of directory DIR without a configuration
         file (logs go to the terminal). DIR can also be given as a plain
         positional argument: "ocsigenserver ./public".
         Files and directories whose name starts with a dot (.git, .env, ...)
         are neither served nor listed, except /.well-known/.

  --reverse-proxy URL
         Forward every request to the base URL given (for example
         http://localhost:9000), without a configuration file.

  -P, --port
         Port to listen on in serve mode or with --reverse-proxy (default
         8080).

  --address ADDR
         IP address to listen on in serve mode or with --reverse-proxy
         (default 127.0.0.1, so the server is only reachable from the same
         machine). Use 0.0.0.0 to listen on all the IPv4 interfaces, for
         example in a container.

  --directory-listing
         In serve mode, list the contents of directories that have no
         index file.

  --max-request-body-size SIZE
         With --reverse-proxy, the maximum size of a request body, as
         <maxrequestbodysize> in a configuration file: 100MB, 1GiB, or
         infinity for no limit. A larger body gets a 413. Default: 8MB.

  -c, --config
         Alternate configuration file.

  -d, --daemon
         Daemon mode (detach the process).
         Implies -s, so it needs log files or syslog.
         The command returns once the server is initialised, and fails
         with its error and exit code if it does not start.

  -help, --help
         Show summary of options.

  -p, --pidfile
         Specify a file where to write the PID of the server.

  -s, --silent
         Silent mode (error messages go in errors.log only). Refused when
         the logs go to the terminal, as in serve mode and with
         --reverse-proxy.

  -v, --verbose
         Show the warnings, which the server already does by default.
         Kept for compatibility.

  -vv, --veryverbose
         Very verbose mode (info).

  -vvv
         Extremely verbose mode (debug).

  --version
         Show version of program.
v}





